ementation integrates the\n   Visibility API and handles 403 gracefully by design — the strategy's acceptance\n   criteria explicitly cover BOTH outcomes (position shown with RBAC; silently omitted\n   without). The only thing the answer changes is how much UI polish is invested in the\n   position display. Captured as an acceptance criterion on E003 (validate default RBAC\n   in an RHOAI cluster and record whether position display is user-facing or effectively\n   admin-only) and as a health warning below.\n2. **Form of the positive Kueue indicator** (owner: UXD, RHOAIUX-7470). Engineering effort\n   is minimal regardless of choice; Staff Input mandates placeholder visual treatment\n   during development. Captured as a placeholder-copy acceptance criterion on E002, with\n   the final treatment delivered by E005.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Visibility API RBAC uncertainty (non-blocking):** if data scientists in default RHOAI\n  clusters do not receive `view`/`edit`/`batch-user` role bindings that aggregate Kueue's\n  `pending-workloads-lq-viewer-role`, queue position is effectively admin-only and the\n  position UI polish investment should be reduced. The code path is identical either way\n  (graceful 403), so this is a scope-polish risk, not a structural gate.\n- **Scope trap in AI-generated section:** the AI-generated \"Proposed Solution\" promises\n  resource quota visibility (\"requested vs. available\"), estimated wait times, queue\n  policy details, and historical wait-time patterns. These are excluded by Staff Input\n  scope constraints (LocalQueues carry no quota; ClusterQueue RBAC unavailable to users;\n  Kueue has no wait-time prediction) and by the Out of Scope list. Only the committed HLR\n  list was decomposed. Reviewers should not treat the Proposed Solution section as scope.\n- **Content dependency timing (non-blocking):** status message microcopy and indicator\n  visual treatment depend on RHOAIUX-7470 delivery, which is outside this pipeline's\n  control. Engineering epics ship placeholder defaults so this cannot block the schedule;\n  the content epic replaces placeholders on delivery.\n- **Backwards-compatibility risk on the status model:** E001 rewrites the status\n  derivation from event-driven progress steps to condition-driven derivation. The\n  strategy requires purely additive, conditional behavior when Kueue is not enabled —\n  E001 carries an acceptance criterion to verify unchanged status display for\n  non-Kueue workloads.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. There is no external dependency with uncertain timing that gates\nuser-facing value: the Kueue Visibility API ships in RHBoK with RHOAI 3.5 (confirmed\nassumption), and the UXD content dependency is explicitly non-blocking via placeholder\ncopy. All engineering epics deliver independent value in parallel.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":34318,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a60dde69fe10feb4","agent_id":"a4bc18f31254a1faa","session_id":"c707f99e-2a7a-41d1-b409-ac9a210055ae","uuid":"210be427-75f7-42ef-8935-d4146dd4e7c5","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:51:29.399Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8002 into epics"}
