{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc899e75c03d7097","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8002\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# RHAISTRAT-8002 Decomposition — Kueue Workload Scheduling Visibility in Workbenches Overview\n10\t\n11\t## Triage\n12\t\n13\tNot below-threshold: although the effort estimate is S and all engineering work is\n14\tbounded to a single component (odh-dashboard), the strategy spans multiple architecturally\n15\tdistinct work streams that warrant separate review cycles — frontend code implementation\n16\t(Dashboard UI), substantive UX/content authoring for status message microcopy and indicator\n17\tvisual treatment (RHOAIUX-7470, UXD + Content), and documentation (release note and\n18\tpossibly feature docs — the strategy declares Documentation Support Required). Multiple\n19\timpacted teams (Dashboard UI, UXD, Content, Docs) confirm the split. Not docs-only:\n20\todh-dashboard requires active code changes.\n21\t\n22\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n23\t\n24\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section takes precedence over the AI-generated strategy\n25\tbody. Overrides applied:\n26\t\n27\t1. **rhods-operator removed from scope.** The HardwareProfile mutating webhook that injects\n28\t   the `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name` label is explicitly not part of this STRAT. All work is\n29\t   odh-dashboard frontend.\n30\t2. **Dependencies table replaced.** The only real dependency is UXD finalized designs and\n31\t   status message content (RHOAIUX-7470), and development must NOT be blocked on it —\n32\t   placeholder copy is used during development and replaced when UX delivers. No other\n33\t   dependencies; the remaining items are treated as assumptions.\n34\t3. **AI-generated \"Proposed Solution\" scope curtailed.** The AI-generated section suggests\n35\t   resource quota visibility (\"requested vs. available\"), queue policy views, historical\n36\t   wait-time patterns, and estimated wait times. The Staff Input scope constraints and\n37\t   committed HLR list exclude these (LocalQueues carry no quota information; users may\n38\t   lack RBAC to read ClusterQueues; Kueue provides no wait-time predictions). Only the\n39\t   committed HLRs are decomposed.\n40\t\n41\t## Component Mapping\n42\t\n43\tThe strategy names the component `odh-dashboard`. The canonical RHAI Jira component list\n44\thas no literal `odh-dashboard` entry; the closest canonical name is **AI Core Dashboard**,\n45\twhich covers the odh-dashboard repository (frontend + BFF). All Dashboard UI epics use\n46\t`AI Core Dashboard`. Non-obvious mappings:\n47\t\n48\t- `odh-dashboard` (strategy) → `AI Core Dashboard` (canonical) — all four engineering epics.\n49\t- UXD deliverable epic → `UXD` (canonical).\n50\t- Documentation epic → `Documentation` (canonical).\n51\t\n52\t## Epic List\n53\t\n54\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n55\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n56\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E001 | Structured Kueue workload status model with eviction-reason separation and message overrides | Implementation | Dashboard UI | P0 |\n57\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E002 | Kueue management indicators: positive project indicator and non-Kueue workload anomaly indicator | Implementation | Dashboard UI | P0 |\n58\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E003 | Kueue Visibility API queue-position integration with graceful RBAC degradation | Implementation | Dashboard UI | P1 |\n59\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E004 | HardwareProfile queue-name validation, GitOps popover fallback, and dropdown explanation | Implementation | Dashboard UI | P1 |\n60\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E005 | Kueue status message microcopy and indicator visual treatment (RHOAIUX-7470) | Implementation | UXD | P1 |\n61\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E006 | Workbenches Kueue visibility documentation and release note | Implementation | Documentation | P1 |\n62\t\n63\t## Dependency DAG\n64\t\n65\t```mermaid\n66\tgraph TD\n67\t    E001[\"E001 Status model (P0)\"] --> E005[\"E005 UX content & visual treatment (P1)\"]\n68\t    E002[\"E002 Kueue indicators (P0)\"] --> E005\n69\t    E001 --> E006[\"E006 Documentation (P1)\"]\n70\t    E002 --> E006\n71\t    E003[\"E003 Visibility API position (P1)\"] --> E006\n72\t    E004[\"E004 HP form & popover (P1)\"] --> E006\n73\t    E005 --> E006\n74\t```\n75\t\n76\t## DAG Justification\n77\t\n78\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n79\t|------|------|-----------|\n80\t| E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003 ∥ E004 (no edges) | Rule 15 | Same repo (odh-dashboard), different areas: status derivation logic vs. project-details/overview indicators vs. Visibility API data fetching vs. HardwareProfile forms. No epic builds an artifact another builds against; shared UI surfaces are extended additively, coordination risk only. |\n81\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 12 (analog) | The status message override content replaces the placeholder defaults shipped with the structured status model; content review needs the implemented override table and final condition/reason set to review against. |\n82\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 12 (analog) | Indicator visual treatment finalizes the placeholder-styled positive and anomaly indicators once the surfaces exist. |\n83\t| E001 → E006, E002 → E006, E003 → E006, E004 → E006, E005 → E006 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by ALL Implementation epics in the strategy; docs describe what was built, including the final UX content. |\n84\t\n85\tNo Investigation epics: see \"Open Questions Handling\" below.\n86\t\n87\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n88\t\n89\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abbreviated) | Epic(s) |\n90\t|-----|----------|---------------------------|---------|\n91\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Distinguish Preempted from other Eviction reasons in workbench status | E001 |\n92\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Surface Requeued state with retry count and next retry time from `requeueState` | E001 |\n93\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Positive Kueue indicator on project details when namespace has `kueue.openshift.io/managed=true` | E002 |\n94\t| HLR-4 | P0 | Anomaly indicator on non-Kueue workloads in Kueue-managed namespaces | E002 |\n95\t| HLR-5 | P0 | Scheduling status from Workload CR conditions with human-friendly overrides and raw-message fallback | E001, E005 |\n96\t| HLR-6 | P1 | Kueue Visibility API queue position via `PendingWorkloadsSummary`, graceful 403 | E003 |\n97\t| HLR-7 | P1 | HardwareProfile popover fallback for GitOps-managed notebooks with queue-label source indication | E004 |\n98\t| HLR-8 | P1 | `k8sNameRegex` validation on LocalQueue name in HardwareProfile admin form | E004 |\n99\t| HLR-9 | P1 | Info indicator explaining why only queue-enabled HPs are shown | E004 |\n100\t| HLR-10 | P2 | Structured, extensible status derivation model (away from wildcard/substring event matching) | E001 |\n101\t| HLR-11 | P2 | Status message granularity: WaitForRecovery vs. WaitForStart, AdmissionCheck pending/retry | E001 |\n102\t\n103\tFull coverage: all 11 HLRs map to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n104\t\n105\t**Priority bundling note (HLR-10, HLR-11):** the two P2 HLRs are bundled into E001 rather\n106\tthan split into a separate P2 epic. They share the same data structures (Workload CR\n107\tconditions), the same type union (`WorkloadCondition` in `k8sTypes.ts`), and the same UI\n108\tsurface (workbench status display) as the P0 status HLRs — HLR-10 is in fact the\n109\tfoundation the P0 derivations build on. They are not independently deferrable features;\n110\tsplitting them would create two epics building the same status-derivation surface plus an\n111\tartificial serialization edge.\n112\t\n113\t**E005 priority note:** although E005 refines HLR-5 (P0) content, the strategy explicitly\n114\tdirects engineering to proceed with placeholder copy and replace it when UX delivers\n115\t(RHOAIUX-7470), so the content finalization is a deferrable, non-blocking follow-on — P1.\n116\tE006 (docs-authoring) derives P1 from the strategy frontmatter priority `Major`\n117\t(docs-authoring priority exception).\n118\t\n119\t## Open Questions Handling\n120\t\n121\tThe strategy leaves exactly two open questions; neither warrants an Investigation epic:\n122\t\n123\t1. **Visibility API RBAC for data scientists** (owner: AI Core Platform). The answer does\n124\t   not change which epics exist or what they build: the implementation integrates the\n125\t   Visibility API and handles 403 gracefully by design — the strategy's acceptance\n126\t   criteria explicitly cover BOTH outcomes (position shown with RBAC; silently omitted\n127\t   without). The only thing the answer changes is how much UI polish is invested in the\n128\t   position display. Captured as an acceptance criterion on E003 (validate default RBAC\n129\t   in an RHOAI cluster and record whether position display is user-facing or effectively\n130\t   admin-only) and as a health warning below.\n131\t2. **Form of the positive Kueue indicator** (owner: UXD, RHOAIUX-7470). Engineering effort\n132\t   is minimal regardless of choice; Staff Input mandates placeholder visual treatment\n133\t   during development. Captured as a placeholder-copy acceptance criterion on E002, with\n134\t   the final treatment delivered by E005.\n135\t\n136\t## Health Warnings\n137\t\n138\t- **Visibility API RBAC uncertainty (non-blocking):** if data scientists in default RHOAI\n139\t  clusters do not receive `view`/`edit`/`batch-user` role bindings that aggregate Kueue's\n140\t  `pending-workloads-lq-viewer-role`, queue position is effectively admin-only and the\n141\t  position UI polish investment should be reduced. The code path is identical either way\n142\t  (graceful 403), so this is a scope-polish risk, not a structural gate.\n143\t- **Scope trap in AI-generated section:** the AI-generated \"Proposed Solution\" promises\n144\t  resource quota visibility (\"requested vs. available\"), estimated wait times, queue\n145\t  policy details, and